Stop macOS from replacing the frame title with the frame size while resizing.

While a window edge is being dragged, -[EmacsView windowWillResize:toSize:]
saves the NSWindow title and puts the text-grid dimensions in its place, so
the title bar reads "*Org Agenda*  —  (124 × 57)" until the drag ends and
-[EmacsView viewDidEndLiveResize] restores what was there before.

This is unconditional: it is not driven by `frame-title-format' or by any
other Lisp variable, and re-setting the title from Lisp cannot win, because
Emacs only pushes a title to AppKit when the *computed* string changes while
this code overwrites the NSWindow title on every step of the resize.  Under
the GNUstep port the window manager shows the size itself, which is where the
behaviour comes from; macOS window managers do not, and a title that changes
under the pointer is more distracting than the size is useful.

Remove the block.  `old_title' is then never set, which leaves
viewDidEndLiveResize's restore a no-op while it still clears
`maximizing_resize', so nothing else in the resize path changes.

--- a/src/nsterm.m
+++ b/src/nsterm.m
@@ -7834,39 +7834,6 @@
                                            frameSize.height - extra);
   if (rows < MINHEIGHT)
     rows = MINHEIGHT;
